WEBVTT

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:04.037
Percentiles tell us where values fall within a distribution.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:10.417
On this slide, we calculate the twenty-fifth, fiftieth, and seventy-fifth percentiles.

00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:14.282
The twenty-fifth percentile is often called Q one.

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:19.787
The fiftieth percentile is the median, and the seventy-fifth percentile is Q three.

00:00:19.787 --> 00:00:22.537
Click Run and inspect these values.

00:00:22.537 --> 00:00:29.452
We also calculate the interquartile range, or IQR, by subtracting Q one from Q three.

00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:34.152
The IQR describes the spread of the middle fifty percent of the data.

00:00:34.152 --> 00:00:39.777
It is often used in box plots and in simple rules for flagging potential outliers.

00:00:39.777 --> 00:00:43.622
Percentiles are useful when the average alone is not enough.

00:00:43.622 --> 00:00:50.902
For example, income, transaction amounts, response times, and hospital length of stay can all have skewed

00:00:50.902 --> 00:00:52.462
distributions.

00:00:52.462 --> 00:00:59.333
A percentile helps us ask questions such as: what value separates the top twenty-five percent from the rest?
